Correct, Jay.

The feature that matt originally referred to is not anything special that
needs to be done by the programmer (from what I remember in Inside Mac I).
Unfortunately, what matt is describing is currently impossible to do
without mucking around behind the system's back.  If designed correctly, any
app will allow you to move the app's background windows (w/o bringing them to
the front) by holding down the command key. This applies only to the
app's windows. Anytime you click on a window of a different app, that
app will be brought to the front (by Multifinder). So until Apple provides
support for this feature, you won't be seeing many apps doing this type
of thing. Besides I don't have the time to spend with TMON or Macsbug
to check out how MF does it's stuff.

